room      lemma_11
kind      geometry
import    Newton, Principia, Andrew Motte trans., 1729 (Wikisource); Book I, Section I, Lemma XI; Plate 2, Fig. 4.
caption   The evanescent subtense of the angle of contact, in all curves of finite curvature, is ultimately in the duplicate ratio of the subtense of the conterminate arc.
final     3

station 1
  ceiling   eq0 :: \angle BAD \to 0,\quad \text{arc } AB \sim AB \;(\text{Lem. VI, VII})
  gloss   The smooth curve through A with its tangent AD; a point B slides along the arc toward A.
  color   tanblue   #1E6FE0
  color   arcgreen  #00A35A
  panel
    point    A   @(0,0)    marker=dot label=$A$ at=SW
    point    ptC @(1.8,1.4) label=$C$ at=NW
    point    B   @(3.4,2.3) marker=dot label=$B$ at=NE
    point    D   @(5.4,0)   marker=dot label=$D$ at=E
    polyline arc A ptC B    color=arcgreen
    segment  tanAD A D      color=tanblue heart label=$AD$ at=SE
    segment  chordAB A B    color=arcgreen label=$AB$ at=NW
  text
    Lemmas VI and VII told us that as $B$ slides toward $A$ the {arcgreen|arc $AB$} \
    and its chord merge with {tanblue|the tangent $AD$}, and the angle between them \
    vanishes. But that only says the gap closes — it does not say \textit{how fast}. \
    The deep question of curvature is: as the small distance $AB$ shrinks, by what \
    power does the curve peel away from its {tanblue|flat tangent}? Everything about \
    force and orbit in the later Propositions rests on the answer, so we measure the \
    departure of the {arcgreen|curve} from the line it kisses at $A$.

station 2
  ceiling   eq1 :: A,B,G \text{ concyclic on diam. } AG \;\Rightarrow\; AB^{2}=AG\times BD
  gloss   BD is the subtense of the angle of contact; the auxiliary BG, AG build a right triangle inscribed in a circle.
  color   tanblue   #1E6FE0
  color   arcgreen  #00A35A
  color   subred    #D81B60
  color   auxpurple #8E24AA
  panel
    point    A   @(0,0)    marker=dot label=$A$ at=SW
    point    ptC @(1.8,1.4) label=$C$ at=NW
    point    B   @(3.4,2.3) marker=dot label=$B$ at=NE
    point    D   @(5.4,0)   marker=dot label=$D$ at=E
    point    Dfoot @(3.4,0) marker=dot label=$D$ at=S
    point    G   @(0.55,4.05) marker=dot label=$G$ at=N
    point    J   @(0.5,3.7)  marker=dot label=$J$ at=W
    polyline arc A ptC B    color=arcgreen
    segment  tanAD A D      color=tanblue
    segment  chordAB A B    color=arcgreen
    segment  subBD B Dfoot  color=subred heart label=$BD$ at=E
    segment  auxBG B G      color=auxpurple label=$BG$ at=NE
    segment  auxAG A G      color=auxpurple label=$AG$ at=W
    circle_3 aux A B G      color=auxpurple
  text
    Drop {subred|$BD$ perpendicular to the tangent} — this is the "subtense of the \
    angle of contact," the precise measure of how far the {arcgreen|curve} has risen \
    above {tanblue|$AD$}. Now the clever auxiliary: draw $BG\perp AB$ and $AG\perp AD$, \
    meeting at $G$. Because both $\angle ABG$ and $\angle AGD$ are right, the points \
    $A,B,G$ lie on {auxpurple|a circle with $AG$ as its diameter}. A chord dropped \
    from that circle gives the exact relation $AB^{2}=AG\times BD$ — trapping the tiny \
    {subred|departure $BD$} inside a clean, computable ratio.

station 3
  ceiling   eq2 :: GJ\to 0 \Rightarrow \frac{AG}{Ag}\to 1 \Rightarrow \frac{AB^{2}}{Ab^{2}}=\frac{BD}{bd}
  gloss   As B→A, GJ vanishes and AG/Ag→1, so AB² : Ab² becomes the duplicate ratio of BD : bd.
  color   tanblue   #1E6FE0
  color   arcgreen  #00A35A
  color   subred    #D81B60
  color   auxpurple #8E24AA
  color   relorange #E8770A
  panel
    point    A   @(0,0)    marker=dot label=$A$ at=SW
    point    B   @(3.4,2.3) marker=dot label=$B$ at=NE
    point    D   @(5.4,0)   marker=dot label=$D$ at=E
    point    Dfoot @(3.4,0) marker=dot label=$D$ at=S
    point    G   @(0.55,4.05) marker=dot label=$G$ at=N
    point    J   @(0.5,3.7)  marker=dot label=$J$ at=W
    point    ptC @(1.8,1.4)
    polyline arc A ptC B    color=arcgreen
    segment  tanAD A D      color=tanblue
    segment  chordAB A B    color=relorange
    segment  subBD B Dfoot  color=relorange
    segment  auxAG A G      color=auxpurple label=$AG$ at=W
    segment  auxGJ G J      color=relorange heart label=$GJ$ at=W
    circle_3 aux A B G      color=auxpurple
  text
    Now shrink everything: let $B,D,G$ approach $A$. The intersection $J$ of $BG$ and \
    $AG$ races toward $A$ so that {relorange|$GJ$ becomes less than any assignable \
    length}, whence $AG:Ag$ differs from equality by nothing in the limit. With \
    {relorange|$AB^{2}=AG\times BD$} and $Ab^{2}=Ag\times bd$, the ratio $AB^{2}:Ab^{2}$ \
    is compounded of $AG:Ag$ and $BD:bd$; since the first factor becomes unity, by \
    Lemma I the ultimate ratio $AB^{2}:Ab^{2}$ equals $BD:bd$. Thus the {subred|subtense} \
    goes as the \textit{square} of the {arcgreen|arc} — every smooth bend, circle or \
    parabola alike, peels from its {tanblue|tangent} quadratically, the universal \
    signature of finite curvature. \textit{Q.E.D.}
